/-
# PERMUTATION GROUP BASE is NP-complete

Source:
K. D. Blaha, "Minimum bases for permutation groups: the greedy approximation", Journal of
Algorithms 13 (1992), 297-306, doi:10.1016/0196-6774(92)90020-D;
P. Crescenzi and V. Kann (eds.), *A compendium of NP optimization problems*, problem MINIMUM
PERMUTATION GROUP BASE (section Miscellaneous).
-/
module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Defs
public import CSLibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.PermutationGroupBaseDefs

@[expose] public section

namespace MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.PermutationGroupBase

/-!
# PERMUTATION GROUP BASE is NP-complete

INSTANCE: a permutation group `G ≤ Sym(n)` on `n` letters, given by generators, and a positive
integer `K ≤ n`. QUESTION: is there a base for `G` of size at most `K`, i.e., a sequence of points
`b₁, ..., bₖ` with `k ≤ K` such that the only element of `G` fixing all of the `bᵢ` is the
identity?

The letters are `0, ..., n - 1` (`Fin n`). A generator is the list `[g 0, ..., g (n - 1)]` of its
images, and `G` is the subgroup of `Equiv.Perm (Fin n)` generated by the generators
(`Subgroup.closure`; with no generators `G` is trivial). The elements of `G` fixing every point of
a set `B` form `G ⊓ fixingSubgroup (Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) B` (Mathlib's pointwise stabilizer), so `B`
is a base exactly when this subgroup is trivial. A base is taken as a finite set of points: the
pointwise stabilizer of a sequence depends only on the set of its points, and dropping repeated
points does not lengthen it.

Encoding: the instance is decoded from a bitstring with the repository's
canonical `MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding`. Undecodable bitstrings, and
bitstrings decoding to malformed instances, are no-instances.

NP-completeness is stated in the same form as the proved results in
`CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Karp`: membership in
`MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP`, plus a polynomial-time many-one
reduction from every language in `NP`
(`Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed`).
-/

/-- **PERMUTATION GROUP BASE is NP-complete.** Source: Blaha (1992); Crescenzi and Kann,
compendium. -/
public theorem_wanted permutationGroupBase_npComplete :
    permutationGroupBaseDec ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP ∧
      ∀ L : MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem,
        L ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP →
          Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed L permutationGroupBaseDec

end MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.PermutationGroupBase
